# Evidence fixtures must retain their captured bytes across platforms. Any line-ending
# conversion would change the artifact and break the digests recorded in
# manifests/sources/*.json, which the fixture tests assert byte for byte.
fixtures/** -text
# The directory's own prose is documentation, not an artifact, so it is normalised.
fixtures/README.md text eol=lf

# Compilation artifacts are content-addressed the same way: an approved control pins one by
# SHA-256 and the resolver hashes the file's bytes to prove it is the artifact named.
#
# These particular artifacts are single-line canonical JSON and so contain no newlines to
# convert — the rule is here because the next artifact format may, and content whose digest
# is load-bearing should never be subject to translation in the first place.
data/compilations/** -text

# The frozen approval ledger beside the tests is the same kind of object, and was not covered
# here until it disagreed with reality. `core.autocrlf` is true on the authoring machine, so
# this file was checked out with LF while the ledger the live sequence-1 report was signed
# under held CRLF — identical text, different bytes, different digest. Tests threading it into
# a digest-checked boundary passed only because they compared it against itself.
tests/historical_pack.json -text

# The published bundles the site serves for download. `/verify` prints the sha256 of this
# exact file and tells a reader to check it, so the bytes are the claim. Without this rule the
# working tree held CRLF (sha 914ea892…) while the committed blob held LF (sha 0b9be60b…), and
# a deploy from a fresh clone would have served a file contradicting the hash printed beside
# its own download link.
site/data/** -text
# The same rule for the rebuilt site, which is the one actually deployed. This was missed when
# `site2/` replaced `site/`: the working tree held the bundle as CRLF (sha 914ea892…, which is
# what the workspace produced, what `/verify` prints and what the host serves) while the
# committed blob was normalised to LF (sha 0b9be60b…). Nothing was wrong on the deploying
# machine, which is what made it dangerous — a clone on any other platform would have served a
# file contradicting the digest printed beside its own download link.
site2/data/** -text

# Everything else `scripts/build_release.py` hashes into a release manifest. It reads bytes
# off disk (`sha256_file`), so any path it digests and `.gitattributes` does not cover has a
# digest that depends on which platform checked the tree out: `manifests/sources` and the
# deployment schema were `i/lf w/crlf`, so a release built on Linux and one built here would
# have disagreed about files neither had changed. `manifests/sources` is covered as a
# directory because `_file_digests` walks the whole tree, not a fixed list.
manifests/sources/** -text
deployments/manifest.schema.json -text
contracts/solidity.json -text
contracts/package-lock.json -text

# systemd units are consumed by Linux, and a unit checked out with CRLF carries a trailing
# carriage return on every value. `ExecStart=/opt/.../python ...` is a command that does not
# exist, so the unit fails at start with a path error naming a binary that looks correct. This
# is the third place in this repository where line endings were load-bearing; the first two
# were caught after the fact, and this one was caught by git's own warning on the way in.
deploy/systemd/** text eol=lf
# The nginx vhost is read by the same kind of consumer for the same reason.
deploy/nginx/** text eol=lf

# The vendored ethers build is content the /app page names by exact version and readers may
# hash against the upstream release. Byte identity beats normalisation for the same reason
# as every rule above; the license text travels with it.
site2/assets/vendor/** -text
